  "narration": {
    "title": "Caminero Catches Fire While the Aces Cool",
    "intro": "This is a week of small swings and one loud bat. Junior Caminero went on a five-homer tear to crash the elite tier, while a couple of front-line arms lost their footing. Everywhere else, the moves were quiet.",
    "risers_lead": "Junior Caminero is the story: his OPS climbed to .930 and he tacked on five home runs in a week, the biggest jump on the board. James Wood kept pace too, up to a .911 OPS with two more long balls for Washington.",
    "pfallers_lead": "Paul Skenes took the hardest fall, shedding 12 points of form to sit at 68, with Joe Ryan (65) and Cam Schlittler (87, down seven) also cooling. Schlittler is still elite even after the dip; the other two just have some ground to make up.",
    "hweek_lead": "Esmerlyn Valdez owned the week for Pittsburgh, going 13-for-24 with four homers and nine RBI for a 1.794 OPS. Milwaukee's Garrett Mitchell wasn't far off at 1.681, and Luis Garc\u00eda Jr. drove in ten runs in six games for the Nationals.",
    "fallers_lead": "Nobody cratered, but Casey Schmitt had the roughest go, a 1-for-20 week that trimmed his season OPS by 47 points. Drake Baldwin (3-for-23) and Jung Hoo Lee slipped a touch too \u2014 cold spells, not collapses.",
    "colors_lead": "Two tiers changed hands, both downward. Corbin Carroll slid from elite to above-average, and Andre Pallante dropped from above-average to average. Modest steps, but worth marking."
  }
}